# ChainMind — AI explorer for Robinhood Chain.
# Copy to `.env.local`; never commit real values.

# --- Groq (LLM) — REQUIRED to answer questions. https://console.groq.com/ ---
GROQ_API_KEY=
# Optional model override (default: llama-3.3-70b-versatile). A smaller model
# such as llama-3.1-8b-instant uses fewer tokens per day on the free tier.
# GROQ_MODEL=

# --- Robinhood Chain (EVM L2) ---
# Network selector: mainnet (chain 4663) | testnet (chain 46630). Default: mainnet.
# ROBINHOOD_NETWORK=mainnet
# Optional dedicated RPC (QuickNode / Chainstack / Alchemy) for higher rate
# limits. Falls back to the public RPC when unset.
# ROBINHOOD_RPC_URL=

# --- Alchemy (OPTIONAL) — all three unset = public RPC, which works fine ---
# Precedence: ROBINHOOD_RPC_URL > ALCHEMY_RPC_URL > template+key > public RPC.
# Preferred: paste the full endpoint URL from the Alchemy dashboard (it already
# contains the key). Used verbatim, so no hostname is ever guessed for you.
# ALCHEMY_RPC_URL=
# Alternative: keep the key out of the URL. BOTH of these must be set — the
# template supplies the host Alchemy assigns to Robinhood Chain, `{key}` is
# replaced by ALCHEMY_API_KEY (no placeholder = key appended as a path segment).
# ALCHEMY_RPC_TEMPLATE=https://<alchemy-host-for-chain-4663>/v2/{key}
# ALCHEMY_API_KEY=

# --- Blockscout (indexer / explorer) ---
# Override the indexer host or add a Pro key to raise rate limits. Defaults to
# the public Robinhood Chain Blockscout instance.
# BLOCKSCOUT_API_URL=https://robinhoodchain.blockscout.com/api
# BLOCKSCOUT_API_KEY=

# Optional: how long a successful indexer response is reused before the same URL
# is fetched again. Milliseconds; default 60000 (1 min). 0 disables storage —
# every read goes upstream — while still collapsing concurrent identical calls
# into one. In-memory and per-instance: on serverless each lambda has its own.
# Failures are never cached at any setting.
# INDEXER_CACHE_TTL_MS=60000

# Optional: the same, for the one indexer read that carries price, market cap and
# 24h volume (/tokens/{address}). Milliseconds; default 10000 (10s). A holder
# count or a verification flag is fine for a minute; an exchange rate is not.
# 0 sends every price read upstream. This is a CEILING, not an override: it can
# only shorten INDEXER_CACHE_TTL_MS, so setting that to 0 still disables both.
# It does not make a figure fresher than the indexer publishes it — that floor is
# upstream — it only stops us adding a minute of our own on top.
# INDEXER_PRICE_CACHE_TTL_MS=10000

# Optional: how long the in-memory tokenized-equity list is reused before the
# token pages are walked again. Milliseconds; default 300000 (5 min). 0 disables
# the cache (every request re-walks Blockscout — only useful for debugging).
# STOCK_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000

# --- Public origin — SECURITY-RELEVANT, and REQUIRED for wallet sign-in ---
# The domain baked into the message a user signs. It is taken from HERE and from
# nowhere else: Host and X-Forwarded-Host are written by whoever is calling, so a
# domain read from them is a domain an ATTACKER picks — they make this app mint a
# challenge naming their site, have the victim sign it there, and redeem that
# signature here. That is the cross-site signature replay the domain binding
# exists to prevent, so the binding cannot be built out of a header.
#
# Leave both unset and wallet sign-in FAILS CLOSED: /api/auth/nonce and
# /api/auth/verify return 503 "not configured" and nobody can sign in. That is
# deliberate — an unconfigured deployment must be unable to sign anyone in rather
# than able to do it unsafely. Everything else in the app still works.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL is also the stable origin for self-calls and metadata, and
# is accepted as a same-origin Origin by the /api/ask abuse guard.
# There is deliberately NO development exception: to try sign-in locally, set
# this to http://localhost:3000. One line, and the local path is the same code
# path as production rather than a lenient variant of it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://your-domain.vercel.app

# Overrides the host above for the signed message only. Set it when the domain
# users actually visit is not the one NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL names (a custom domain
# in front of a *.vercel.app, say) — the signed text must say what the user sees
# in the address bar. A bare host or a full origin; a malformed value is treated
# as unset, so a typo breaks sign-in loudly instead of downgrading it quietly.
# AUTH_DOMAIN=chainmind.fun

# --- Caller identity (lib/api-guard.js) — what the free quota is keyed on ---
# X-Forwarded-For is a LIST that each proxy APPENDS to, so its leftmost entry is
# whatever the caller wrote — trusting it means `curl -H "X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4"`
# is an unlimited free tier. The entry that means anything is the one YOUR proxy
# appended, counted from the RIGHT.
#
# How many proxies sit between this process and the internet. Default 1, which is
# the truth on Vercel, Railway, Fly, Render and a plain nginx. Use 2 for
# Cloudflare in front of your own nginx. 0 means "I trust no proxy": the header is
# ignored entirely and every anonymous caller shares one bucket.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# A header your platform SETS (rather than appends to), and which a caller
# therefore cannot forge. Preferred over hop counting when you have one. Vercel is
# detected automatically (x-vercel-forwarded-for); name yours here otherwise —
# cf-connecting-ip on Cloudflare, fly-client-ip on Fly. Get this wrong and you
# have re-opened the hole, which is why it is explicit rather than sniffed.
# TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=cf-connecting-ip
#
# NOTE what this does and does not buy. It stops the daily limit being reset by
# setting a header. It does NOT stop someone rotating exit nodes or connecting a
# fresh wallet — the free tier is a speed bump, not an anti-abuse system, and the
# real cost controls are the per-minute limiter and the upstream budget.

# --- Wallet sign-in (OPTIONAL; the explorer works without it) ---
# Signing key for the session cookie. REQUIRED to turn wallet sign-in on: with it
# unset the auth routes return 503 "not configured" and issue nothing. There is no
# development default on purpose — a fallback secret is a published secret.
# Generate one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Minimum 32 characters. Rotating it invalidates every existing session.
# SESSION_SECRET=

# How long a session lasts, in ms. Default 86400000 (24h). Sessions are stateless
# signed cookies, so a copied cookie stays valid until it expires — this is the
# only thing bounding that, which is why it is a day and not a month.
# AUTH_SESSION_TTL_MS=86400000

# How long a sign-in challenge stays redeemable, in ms. Default 300000 (5 min).
# Single-use regardless: it is consumed on the first verify attempt, successful or
# not. Shorter is safer; too short and a slow hardware-wallet prompt times out.
# AUTH_NONCE_TTL_MS=300000

# How long the pre-session cookie a challenge is bound to lasts, in ms.
# Default 900000 (15 min).
# AUTH_PRE_SESSION_TTL_MS=900000

# --- Admin page (/admin) — usage dashboard: searches, questions, visitors ---
# The operator password. REQUIRED to enable /admin: unset and the page and its
# API deny everyone. Signing reuses SESSION_SECRET, so that must be set too.
# Choose a long random value; it is compared in constant time.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('base64url'))"
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# How long an admin login lasts, in ms. Default 43200000 (12h).
# ADMIN_SESSION_TTL_MS=43200000
#
# NOTE: usage stats persist only when a durable store is configured (below). With
# the memory driver the admin page still works but its numbers are per-instance
# and reset on deploy — it says so at the top when that is the case.

# --- Store (lib/store.js) — where nonces, quotas and saved history live ---
#
# CONFIGURE ONE OF THESE IN PRODUCTION OR THE DAILY QUOTA IS NOT A LIMIT. With no
# shared store, each instance counts to five on its own — five per lambda, reset
# on every deploy — and sign-in nonces issued by one instance are unknown to the
# next. /api/health reports `quota.enforced: false` and the server logs a banner
# at startup when that is the state you are in.
#
# --- RECOMMENDED: Upstash-compatible Redis over HTTP (NOTHING TO INSTALL) ---
# Both halves are required. Works identically on Vercel (many short-lived
# instances) and Railway (one long process), because every call is a stateless
# HTTPS POST — no TCP pool, no driver, no sixth dependency. Vercel KV is the same
# service: KV_REST_API_URL / KV_REST_API_TOKEN are accepted as aliases.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://your-db.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Driver: memory | redis | postgres. Leave unset and it is inferred:
#   a REST URL + token above    -> redis
#   otherwise a connection string -> postgres
#   neither, dev                -> memory
#   neither, PROD               -> UNCONFIGURED, and every stateful feature FAILS
#                                  CLOSED with a "configure a store" error.
# That last line is deliberate. Production never falls back to memory by accident
# — set STORE_DRIVER=memory explicitly if you are on a single long-lived process
# and accept losing everything on restart AND an unenforced quota.
# STORE_DRIVER=

# How long ONE store round trip may take, in ms. Default 1500. This runs BEFORE
# /api/ask opens its 24s answer budget, so a store with no deadline does not merely
# slow a request down — it spends the answer's time and produces the 504 the budget
# exists to prevent. On timeout the gate degrades to the per-process limiter and
# marks the report `degraded`, rather than blocking or refusing the question.
# 0 disables the deadline; do not.
# STORE_TIMEOUT_MS=1500

# --- ALTERNATIVE: postgres, if you already run one ---
# DATABASE_URL is accepted as an alias, which is what Railway and most managed
# providers inject. TLS settings ride in the URL (?sslmode=require). Tables are
# created on first use.
# NOTE: the postgres driver is NOT bundled — this app ships five runtime deps and
# does not add a sixth. Run `npm install pg` in the deployment that needs it;
# without it, STORE_DRIVER=postgres fails closed with an error saying exactly that.
# It never degrades to memory. The REST option above needs no such step, which is
# why it is the one recommended.
# STORE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/database
# STORE_PG_POOL_MAX=4

# --- Token gate (lib/entitlement.js) — who asks without a daily limit ---
# The gating token's contract address on the active network. UNSET = no gate:
# nobody can be verified as a holder, so EVERY visitor gets the free daily
# allowance below. That is the fail-closed direction on purpose — a deploy that
# forgot this must not hand out unlimited access — and /api/health says so under
# statefulFeatures.tokenGate.
# The value below is the TEST token measured on chain 4663: "COVENANT BIO LLM"
# (COVENANT), 18 decimals, 85 holders, 1e27 total supply. It has no indexer price
# and no market cap, so the threshold is a raw TOKEN COUNT with no dollar meaning.
# Both this and the threshold are env-configurable precisely so the real token can
# be swapped in with no code change.
# GATE_TOKEN_ADDRESS=0xde358e0a0afe80c081121bc7e2bf8852fc6827d6

# How many WHOLE TOKENS an address must hold to ask without a daily limit.
# Decimals are handled for you: 1000 means 1000 tokens, never 1000 wei. Default 1.
# GATE_TOKEN_MIN=1000

# Optional: pin the token's decimals and skip one eth_call. A WRONG value rescales
# the threshold by orders of magnitude, so leave it unset unless you are sure —
# unset means decimals() is read from the contract and cached (it is immutable).
# GATE_TOKEN_DECIMALS=18

# Optional: the symbol used in copy ("hold 1000 COVENANT"). Read from the contract
# when unset; a token with no readable symbol just goes unnamed.
# GATE_TOKEN_SYMBOL=

# How long a measured entitlement is reused before the balance is read again, in
# ms. Default 180000 (3 min). A holder can sell right after signing in, so this is
# minutes rather than the session — and 0 disables the cache (an eth_call per
# question). FAILURES ARE NEVER CACHED at any setting: an RPC blip must not become
# minutes of everyone reading as unverifiable.
# GATE_CACHE_TTL_MS=180000

# Deadline for one balance read, in ms. Default 2500. This runs BEFORE the
# expensive part of /api/ask, so a slow RPC must cost a couple of seconds and then
# be reported as "could not verify" — not eat the answer's time budget.
# GATE_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS=2500

# Free questions per UTC DAY for everyone who is not a verified holder — including
# signed-in wallets below the threshold and wallets whose balance could not be
# read. Default 5. 0 makes the gate a hard paywall. The day is a UTC calendar day
# because the server cannot know a visitor's timezone, and "resets at 00:00 UTC"
# is a promise a user can check against a clock (see lib/quota.js).
# FREE_DAILY_QUESTIONS=5

# --- Open access — the daily limit off for EVERYONE (lib/quota.js) ---
# Set while the product is open for public testing. Every caller is reported as
# unlimited, no counters are written and no limit sentence is shown; unset it and
# FREE_DAILY_QUESTIONS above is back in force the same day, with no code change.
#
# It is a SEPARATE switch from the allowance on purpose: FREE_DAILY_QUESTIONS=0 is a
# hard PAYWALL, the exact opposite of removing the limit, so "set it to zero to turn
# it off" would lock every visitor out. Recognised values are 1/true/yes/on — anything
# else, including a typo, leaves the limit ON rather than silently uncapping the app.
# OPEN_ACCESS=1

# --- Saved history (lib/history.js) — a signed-in user's questions ---
# Set to false to switch saving off entirely. Anything already stored stays until
# it ages out or the user deletes it.
# HISTORY_ENABLED=true

# How many entries one wallet keeps. Default 50, hard ceiling 500 (the store's own
# list cap). At the cap the OLDEST entry drops so the newest can be kept — a save
# is never refused, and the UI says "the last N are kept".
# HISTORY_MAX_ITEMS=50

# Longest saved answer, in characters. Default 4000. Anything longer is stored cut
# short with a marker, so a truncated answer never reads as a complete one.
# HISTORY_MAX_ANSWER_CHARS=4000

# How long an entry survives, in ms. Default 7776000000 (90 days); 0 keeps entries
# until the cap pushes them out. History is user content tied to a wallet address,
# and an address is personal data — three-month-old transcripts nobody has opened
# are a liability rather than a feature. NOTE the adapters differ: postgres expires
# each row, while the memory adapter carries one expiry for the whole list.
# HISTORY_TTL_MS=7776000000

# --- Browser render service (lib/render-client.js, services/render/) ---
# A SEPARATE Railway deployment that renders a URL in a headless Chromium and returns
# what a browser sees. Both variables must be set for it to be used at all; with
# neither, lib/render-client.js reports "not configured" and says in words that the
# absence is a fact about THIS DEPLOYMENT and not about any site.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS, measured through this repo's own lib/safe-fetch.js: https://eska.fun/
# answers HTTP 200 with 5,782 bytes of HTML, and stripToText finds FOUR CHARACTERS of
# visible text in it ("ESKA"). The site is client-rendered — everything a person sees is
# painted by JavaScript that an HTTP GET never runs. Through the render service the same
# URL yields 76,266 bytes and 343 characters, in 2.2s. A raw read of a page like that is
# not a thin read; it is a read of a different document than the one anybody looked at.
#
# The public URL of the Railway service, no trailing slash. It is VALIDATED with the
# same SSRF ladder as any other URL: a typo pointing it at an internal address is
# refused rather than fetched.
# RENDER_SERVICE_URL=https://chainmind-render-production.up.railway.app

# The shared secret, identical on both sides. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# It is the ONLY credential the render service holds, on purpose: that service runs a
# browser executing the investigated party's JavaScript, so it is designed so that a
# full compromise of it yields nothing but the ability to render pages. Never give it
# a chain key, an LLM key or a store credential. The service refuses to start if this
# is missing or shorter than 32 characters.
# RENDER_SHARED_SECRET=

# See services/render/README.md for the Railway steps, the service's own variables
# (concurrency, timeouts, viewport, memory), the health check, and the TLS finding
# behind www.ponsfamily.com's UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEEP RESEARCH — the app's half (lib/research-client.js, app/api/research/)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THESE FOUR ARE READ BY THIS APP. With the first two unset, deep investigations are
# simply not offered: /api/research answers `configured: false`, the ask path says in
# words that this DEPLOYMENT cannot do it and nothing about any project, and no button
# appears that would fail when pressed. Not configured is a state, not a breakage.

# The public URL of the Railway research service, no trailing slash. Checked with the
# same policy lib/render-client.js uses for its service — our own infrastructure, so a
# private hostname on a non-default port is allowed, and the cloud metadata endpoint
# is not.
# RESEARCH_SERVICE_URL=https://chainmind-research-production.up.railway.app

# The research service's shared secret. A DIFFERENT value from RENDER_SHARED_SECRET —
# two services, two doors. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# RESEARCH_SHARED_SECRET=

# Deep investigations per UTC day, per wallet. They are metered separately from
# questions and much more tightly, because one job is minutes of wall clock, up to
# 320,000 model tokens, and up to 12MB fetched from THIRD PARTIES WHO DID NOT AGREE TO
# ANY OF IT. That last cost is why even a verified holder has a number here rather than
# no limit: a token balance is not a third party's consent to be crawled.
# Signing in is required either way — a job has an owner, and only that wallet can read
# its report. 0 disables the tier without disabling the other.
# RESEARCH_DAILY_JOBS=1
# RESEARCH_HOLDER_DAILY_JOBS=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEEP RESEARCH SERVICE (services/research) — its OWN variables, set on Railway
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These belong on the RAILWAY service, not here. They are listed in this file because
# this is where somebody looks for "what has to be set", and a deployable whose
# configuration is documented only inside its own directory is a deployable nobody
# configures. Setting them in the Vercel environment does nothing at all.
#
# WHAT IT IS. lib/site-analysis.js reads ONE page, TWICE, inside a 24-second request
# budget. It cannot probe an endpoint it was not given, cannot read a repository, cannot
# search source — and, the real difference, cannot decide what to look at NEXT. The
# research service is that loop: a model with tools, a step budget and a running dossier,
# submitted as a job and delivered as a structured sourced report minutes later.
#
# It holds the Groq key, the store credentials and the render secret, and that is
# defensible ONLY because it never executes third-party code: when a page needs a browser
# it calls services/render over HTTP and the browser stays over there, with its one
# secret. Do not put a browser in that package.
#
#   RESEARCH_SHARED_SECRET    required, >= 32 chars, its own value (NOT the render one)
#   GROQ_API_KEY              required — without a model there is no loop, only a fetcher
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL    required — the job store, same values as this app uses
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN  required
#   RENDER_SERVICE_URL        optional — the browser leg for JavaScript-built pages
#   RENDER_SHARED_SECRET      optional — the RENDER service's secret
#   RESEARCH_GITHUB_TOKEN     optional — READ-ONLY, PUBLIC-SCOPE only. It raises GitHub's
#                             anonymous 60-per-hour limit to 5,000 and reaches nothing
#                             private. A token with any write scope on it is a mistake.
#
# The caps (steps, tool calls, bytes, wall clock, model tokens, per-host requests, how far
# the loop may wander from what was asked about) all have defaults and all have their own
# variables. See services/research/README.md for the full table, the Railway steps, and
# what the report does with a cap that bit.
